Pleural Effusion
Respiratory System
Stony dullness to percussion, Decreased breath sounds, Decreased tactile fremitus

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-035
Reason for Visit: Worsening, severe shortness of breath on minimal exertion and sharp chest pain, requiring urgent assessment to rule out massive fluid accumulation or pulmonary embolism.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient with progressive dyspnea and sharp, localized chest pain who is now unable to lie flat without severe distress. Urgent clinical assessment was necessary to evaluate for a large unilateral pleural effusion, assess respiratory mechanics, and arrange for immediate radiological investigation and drainage before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I can't catch my breath when I walk to the bathroom, and my left lung feels heavy and tight, like it's completely full of fluid.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms have been progressing over the last 2 weeks. The patient reports worsening dyspnea on exertion that has now become distressing at rest. They describe a persistent, dry, non-productive cough. Over the last 48 hours, they have developed sharp, pleuritic chest pain localized to the left lateral chest wall, which worsens on deep inspiration. The patient explicitly notes they are unable to lie flat (orthopnea) as it significantly worsens the sensation of breathlessness and left-sided heaviness.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently restricted to sitting upright in a chair.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of respiratory exhaustion.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 mmHg | HR: 102 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 24 breaths/min | SpO2: 93% on room air | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Alert, visibly tachypneic, sitting upright, splinting the left side of the chest during breathing.
Physical Exam: Chest examination reveals asymmetrical chest expansion, with reduced movement on the left. Percussion demonstrates pronounced stony dullness over the dependent lung zones on the left side (from the base up to the mid-zone). Auscultation reveals markedly decreased to absent breath sounds over this dull area. Decreased tactile fremitus is noted over the effusion. Egophony is present at the superior border of the dullness. The trachea remains central (no massive mediastinal shift).
Environmental Observations: Multiple pillows stacked on the bed, indicating recent severe orthopnea.
Medication Reconciliation: No medications providing relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Pleural Effusion - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Lobar Pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The percussion note is strictly "stony dull" rather than simply dull, tactile fremitus is decreased (it is increased in consolidation), and there is an absence of fever or purulent sputum.

Spontaneous Pneumothorax - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of stony dullness to percussion rather than hyperresonance.

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The physical signs strongly indicate a large volume of fluid (stony dullness, absent breath sounds) rather than clear lungs typically seen in isolated PE, though PE can cause a small reactive effusion.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits the classic, undeniable physical signs of a moderate-to-large unilateral pleural effusion: stony dullness to percussion, decreased breath sounds, and decreased tactile fremitus over the left lower/mid zones. The resulting mechanical compression of the lung parenchyma is causing significant tachypnea, orthopnea, and borderline hypoxia (SpO2 93%). Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would pose an unacceptable risk of progressive respiratory failure or tension physiology if the fluid rapidly accumulates further. An urgent after-hours home visit was strictly required to confirm the mechanical nature of the dyspnea, provide strong analgesia for the pleuritic pain to improve respiratory mechanics, and arrange an immediate, urgent pathway for imaging and therapeutic thoracentesis.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Ibuprofen 400mg and Paracetamol 1000mg to manage the pleuritic pain and reduce splinting. Generated an urgent referral for an erect chest X-ray and respiratory physician review.
Positive Behavioral Change: Sleep strictly propped up on three firm pillows at a 45-degree angle tonight; utilizing gravity to pool the fluid at the bottom of your chest cavity directly frees up the top half of your lung to expand, preventing the severe breathlessness that wakes you up.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Patient must present to the radiology clinic first thing in the morning for the urgent X-ray (looking for a meniscus sign); early GP/specialist review tomorrow to arrange physical drainage of the fluid.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ambulance transfer (call 000) to the Emergency Department if the breathlessness becomes severe while sitting still, if your lips turn blue, or if you feel dizzy or lightheaded upon standing.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: A chest X-ray and an ultrasound are required to confirm the exact amount of fluid, and a sample of the fluid must be drawn with a needle to determine the underlying cause (e.g., infection, heart failure, malignancy).
Clinical Course: The breathlessness will not fully resolve until the fluid is physically drained from the chest cavity by a specialist.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 immediately if you feel you are suffocating, or if the sharp pain spreads to the center of your chest or your jaw.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A large amount of fluid has become trapped between your lung and your ribcage, physically crushing the lung and preventing it from inflating; the pain medication allows you to take deeper breaths, but the fluid itself must be medically drained.
Actionable Steps: Take the prescribed pain relief exactly on schedule, even if the pain feels mild; preventing the sharp pain allows your chest wall to move freely, ensuring the healthy parts of your lung get enough oxygen.

